  "theory": {
    "intro": "Any exponential is integrated by dividing by the natural logarithm of its base: $\\int a^{x}\\,dx = \\dfrac{a^{x}}{\\ln a} + C$ for $a > 0$. Differentiating $a^{x}$ multiplies by $\\ln a$, so integrating divides by it.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\int a^{x}\\,dx = \\dfrac{a^{x}}{\\ln a} + C$ for a constant base $a > 0$ with $a \\ne 1$.",
      "Differentiating $a^{x}$ multiplies by $\\ln a$, so integrating $a^{x}$ divides by $\\ln a$.",
      "Multiplying by $\\ln a$ instead of dividing is the most common error.",
      "$\\int a^{mx+c}\\,dx = \\dfrac{a^{mx+c}}{m\\ln a} + C$, the chain rule contributing the extra divisor $m$.",
      "When $a = e$ the rule collapses to $\\int e^{x}\\,dx = e^{x} + C$, because $\\ln e = 1$.",
      "$\\ln a$ is a fixed number, so $\\dfrac{1}{\\ln a}$ behaves like any other constant factor.",
      "The power rule does not apply here, because $x$ is the index and $a$ is the base.",
      "Since $\\ln a^{k} = k\\ln a$, rewriting the base gives the same primitive in a different form.",
      "For a definite integral, evaluate $\\left[\\dfrac{a^{x}}{\\ln a}\\right]_{p}^{q}$ and leave $\\ln a$ in the exact answer.",
      "Check any primitive by differentiating it back to the integrand."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Write the integrand in the form $ka^{mx+c}$ with base $a > 0$.",
      "Write down $a$ to the same index, unchanged.",
      "Divide by $\\ln a$, and by $m$, the coefficient of $x$ in the index.",
      "Add $+C$ for an indefinite integral, or evaluate $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{p}^{q}$ for a definite one."
    ]
  },
